{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>Basemat daughter of Ishmael</b> — later on he calls her Mahalat (Genesis 28:9). I found in the Aggadah of Midrash Sefer Samuel (chapter 17): three are forgiven for all their iniquities — a convert who converts, one who rises to greatness, and one who marries a wife. And the reason was derived from here: therefore she is called Mahalat, because his iniquities were forgiven him. All this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Genesis 36:3). Now, he gave no reason for the father of Oholivamah, who is called there Beeri and here Anah, and Basemat daughter of Ishmael would be a proper noun, and it is an adjective referring to the burning of spices. And in Bereshit Rabbah (Bereshit Rabbah 67:13) they said: Esau set his mind to convert — Mahalat, because the Holy One, blessed be He, forgave him his iniquities; Basemat, because his mind was perfumed upon it. And behold, both of them are adjectives; the proper noun is not known in them. Therefore Rabbi Abraham said that she had two names, and so did her father. And it is possible to say that those two women died without children, or perhaps they were punished because they were a bitterness of spirit to Isaac and to Rebecca, and he married the sister of his wife, the daughter of Elon, and another, Oholivamah daughter of Anah. But the daughter of Ishmael, the sister of Nevayot — because she had a disparaging name in the sacred tongue, Mahalat, he called her by the honored name that his first wife had, from the root בשמים (spices), for she was beloved to him because she was of his family and she was not evil in the eyes of Isaac his father."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 36, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:40:15.895604106Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}